    "lesson": [
      {
        "t": "h",
        "x": "More about Adjective Clauses"
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "x": "An Adjective Clause in a Complex sentence is a subordinate clause which does the work of an Adjective, and so qualifies some noun or pronoun in the main clause. In this chapter we learn how Adjective Clauses are introduced, and when the Relative Pronoun or Relative Adverb is left out."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 38. An Adjective Clause is introduced by a Relative Pronoun or by a Relative Adverb."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "Uneasy lies the head that wears a crown.\nHe is the man whom we all respect.\nThe time when the boat leaves is not yet fixed.\nThe house where the accident occurred is nearby.\nThe reason why I did it is obvious."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "The boy who sits next to me is very clever. (who introduces the Adjective Clause.) This is the book which I bought yesterday. (which introduces the Adjective Clause.) I know the place where he was born. (where introduces the Adjective Clause.)"
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "x": "A Relative Pronoun Introducing a Co-ordinate Clause"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 39. Sometimes, however, a Relative Pronoun introduces a Co-ordinate clause. I met Rama, who (= and he) gave me your message. Here we are using the Relative Pronoun who to introduce a co-ordinate clause. It might appear, at first sight, that the clause, who gave me your message, is an Adjective clause and therefore Subordinate. It will be seen that this is not the case however, for it in no way identifies or describes Rama. In the sentence, He is the boy who broke the window, the clause, who broke the window, clearly identifies and describes the boy, and is therefore an Adjective clause."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 40. Below are further examples of who (and which) used to introduce a co-ordinate clause. 1. I met Mr. Joshi, who (= and he) thereupon shook hands with me. 2. The prisoner was taken before the Captain, who (= and he) condemned him to instant death. 3. He gave me a message, which (= and it) is this. 4. He released the bird, which (= and it) at once flew away."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "I met Ahmed, who gave me your book. (who = and he; this is a Co-ordinate Clause, not an Adjective Clause, because it does not describe Ahmed.) I saw the teacher, who smiled at me. (who = and he; a Co-ordinate Clause.) But: I saw the teacher who teaches us English. (who teaches us English is an Adjective Clause, because it tells us which teacher.)"
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "x": "The Relative Pronoun or Adverb Understood"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 41. The Relative Pronoun or the Relative Adverb, introducing an Adjective clause, is sometimes understood, and not expressed."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "1. Eat all ^ you can. (Here the Relative Pronoun that is understood.)\n2. I saw a man ^ I know. (Here the Relative Pronoun whom is understood.)\n3. Where's the book ^ he left for me? (Here the Relative Pronoun which is understood.)\n4. On the day ^ you pass the examination I shall give you a reward. (Here the Relative Adverb when is understood.)\n5. The reason ^ I have come is to ask for my money. (Here the relative Adverb why is understood.)"
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "This is the book I bought yesterday. (that is understood.) The man I met in the bazaar is my uncle. (whom is understood.) The day we won the match was a happy day. (when is understood.)"
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "x": "But as a Relative Pronoun"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 42. In older English but was used as a relative pronoun. In such cases but is equivalent to a relative pronoun followed by not."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "1. There was not a woman present but wept to hear such news. (That is, who did not weep to hear such news.)\n2. And not a man of the three hundred at Thermopylae but died at his post. (That is, who did not die at his post.)\n3. Nor is there a man here but loved our Caesar. (That is, who did not love our Caesar.)\n4. There was not a widow but longed to die upon the pyre of her husband. (That is, who did not long to die, etc.)\n5. There is no fireside but has one vacant chair. (That has not one vacant chair.)"
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "x": "Than before a Relative Pronoun"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 43. Note that than is sometimes used as a Preposition before a Relative Pronoun in the Adjective Clause. They elected Rama than whom no better boy ever went to school. We will follow Brutus than whom Rome knows no nobler son. It was a blow than which no crueller was ever struck. We came to a spot than which mine eyes have seldom seen a lovelier sight."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "x": "The Infinitive as an Adjective Clause"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 44. The infinitive with to is often used as the equivalent of an Adjective Clause."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "1. (a) Give me some food which I may eat. (b) Give me some food to eat.\n2. (a) He has no boots which he can wear. (b) He has no boots to wear.\n3. (a) The doctor has given me medicine which I must take. (b) The doctor has given me medicine to take.\n4. (a) I have work which I must do. (b) I have work to do.\n5. (a) His mother gave him a rupee which he might put in his money-box. (b) His mother gave him a rupee to put in his money-box."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "I have no pen to write with. (= which I can write with.) Give me some water to drink. (= which I may drink.) We have a lot of work to do. (= which we must do.)"
      }
    ],
