      {
        "t": "p",
        "x": "We have learnt how to analyse a Simple Sentence. In this chapter we learn how to analyse a Complex Sentence — that is, how to break it into its Clauses and say what kind each Clause is and what work it does."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 57. A Complex sentence consists of one Main Clause and one or more Subordinate Clauses. The Main Clause is the clause which makes good sense by itself. Each Subordinate Clause does the work of a Noun, an Adjective or an Adverb."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 58. In analysing a Complex sentence, first pick out the Main Clause, then pick out each Subordinate Clause and say: (a) what kind of clause it is — Noun, Adjective or Adverb; (b) what work it does — Subject, Object, Complement, in Apposition, Object of a Preposition, qualifying a noun, or modifying a verb, adjective or adverb."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "Example: They rested when evening came. (i) They rested — Main Clause. (ii) when evening came — Adverb Clause of Time, modifying rested."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "We stayed at home because it was raining. (i) We stayed at home — Main Clause. (ii) because it was raining — Adverb Clause of Cause, modifying stayed. I know that Ali will come. (i) I know — Main Clause. (ii) that Ali will come — Noun Clause, Object of know. The boy who won the race is my friend. (i) The boy is my friend — Main Clause. (ii) who won the race — Adjective Clause, qualifying boy."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "x": "How to Analyse — Worked Examples"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 59. Worked example 1: He said that he was innocent. (i) He said — Main Clause. (ii) that he was innocent — Noun Clause, Object of the verb said."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 60. Worked example 2: The house where he was born is still standing. (i) The house is still standing — Main Clause. (ii) where he was born — Adjective Clause, qualifying house."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 61. Worked example 3: If you work hard, you will pass. (i) You will pass — Main Clause. (ii) If you work hard — Adverb Clause of Condition, modifying will pass."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 62. Worked example 4: That he will come is certain. (i) Is certain — Main Clause (with the Noun Clause as Subject). (ii) That he will come — Noun Clause, Subject of the verb is."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "The book which I bought yesterday is very useful. (i) The book is very useful — Main Clause. (ii) which I bought yesterday — Adjective Clause, qualifying book. Tell me where you live. (i) Tell me — Main Clause. (ii) where you live — Noun Clause, Object of tell."
      },
      {
        "t": "note",
        "x": "Note: In a Complex sentence the Main Clause may come first or last. Always find the clause that makes good sense by itself — that is the Main Clause."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "If you work hard, you will pass the examination. (i) You will pass the examination — Main Clause. (ii) If you work hard — Adverb Clause of Condition, modifying will pass. The boy who sits next to me is my best friend. (i) The boy is my best friend — Main Clause. (ii) who sits next to me — Adjective Clause, qualifying boy. I know that our cricket team will win. (i) I know — Main Clause. (ii) that our cricket team will win — Noun Clause, Object of know."
      }
