      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "Real life: What a beautiful day it is! It is a very beautiful day. How fast he runs! He runs very fast. If only I had a bicycle! I wish I had a bicycle."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "x": "7. Interchange of One Part of Speech for Another"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 78. We can change one part of speech into another without changing the meaning. A noun can become a verb, a verb can become a noun, an adjective can become an adverb, and so on."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "1. That kind of joke does not amuse me.\nThat kind of joke does not give me any amusement.\n2. It costs twelve rupees.\nThe cost is twelve rupees.\n3. He has disgraced his family.\nHe is a disgrace to his family.\n4. He fought bravely.\nHe put up a brave fight.\n5. I cannot consent to your going.\nI cannot give my consent to your going.\n6. He gave a curt reply.\nHe replied curtly.\n7. He showed generosity even to his enemies.\nHe was generous even to his enemies.\n8. There is a slight difference between the two shades.\nThe two shades are slightly different.\n9. The Act made the negro slaves free.\nThe Act gave freedom to the negro slaves.\n10. I see him everyday.\nI see him daily.\n11. He examined the document carefully.\nHe examined the document with care.\n12. We passed an anxious hour.\nWe passed an hour anxiously."
      },
      {
        "t": "note",
        "x": "Note: In Exercise 70 the words in italics must be changed. Read the question carefully to see which part of speech the italic word must become."
      }
