    "lesson": [
      {
        "t": "h",
        "x": "The Sequence of Tenses"
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "x": "When a sentence has a Main clause and a Subordinate clause, the tense of the verb in the Subordinate clause must agree with the tense of the verb in the Main clause. This is called the Sequence of Tenses."
      },
      {
        "t": "h",
        "x": "The Rules"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 95. The Sequence of Tenses is the principle in accordance with which the Tense of the verb in a subordinate clause follows the Tense of the verb in the principal clause."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Rule 1. A Past Tense in the principal clause is followed by a Past Tense in the subordinate clause."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "He said that he was ill.\nI knew that he would come.\nShe told me that she had finished her work.\nHe ran because he was afraid."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Rule 2. A Present or Future Tense in the principal clause may be followed by any tense in the subordinate clause."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "He says that he is ill.\nHe says that he was ill.\nHe says that he will be ill.\nI think that he will come tomorrow."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 96. Exceptions. When the subordinate clause states a universal truth or a habitual fact, the Present Tense is used even after a Past Tense in the principal clause."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "He said that the sun rises in the east.\nThe teacher told us that honesty is the best policy.\nHe spoke as one who knows. (He speaks as one who knows.)"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 97. Can, may, will and shall change in the past: can becomes could, may becomes might, will becomes would, shall becomes should."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "He says that he can do it. He said that he could do it.\nHe says that he may come. He said that he might come.\nShe says that she will come. She told me that she would come.\nHe ran as quickly as he could. (Past: could, not can.)\nHe labours hard so that he may become rich. (Present: may.)\nHe laboured hard so that he might become rich. (Past: might.)"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "After 'as though' and 'as if' we use 'were' for all persons: He speaks as though he were very angry."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "Real life: Ali said that he was ill. Ali says that he is ill. The teacher told us that the earth moves round the sun. He said that he could swim. He says that he can swim."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "Real life: I knew that he would come. I know that he will come. She told me that she would help me. She tells me that she will help me. He ran because he was afraid."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "Real life: He worked hard so that he might pass. He works hard so that he may pass. I waited for my friend until he came. I wait for my friend until he comes."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "Real life: He speaks as though he were angry. He spoke as though he were angry. I asked him what I could do. I ask him what I can do. He said that he would try again."
      },
      {
        "t": "note",
        "x": "Note: In purpose clauses (so that, in order that) we use may after a Present Tense and might after a Past Tense."
      }
    ],
