      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "He who has suffered most in the cause, let him speak. (Incorrect)\nLet him who has suffered most in the cause speak. (Correct)\nHe who has suffered most in the cause should speak. (Correct)"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 164. A verb should agree with its subject, and not with the complement."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "What is wanted is (not are) not large houses with modern conveniences, but small cottages.\nThe details are a matter for future consideration."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 165. In a compound sentence a single verb can be made to do duty for two subjects, only when the form of the verb is such as to permit of it."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "Not a drum was heard, not a funeral note [was heard]. (Correct)\nHis diet was abstemious, his prayers long and fervent. (Incorrect — the subjects are not in the same number.)\nHis diet was abstemious, his prayers were long and fervent. (Correct)"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 166. Two auxiliaries can be used with one principal verb, only when the form of the principal verb is appropriate to both the auxiliaries."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "I never have hurt anybody, and never will. (Correct)\nHe never has, and never will, take such strong measures. (Incorrect)\nHe never has taken, and never will take, such strong measures. (Correct)"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 167. When there is only one auxiliary to two principal verbs, it should be such that it may be correctly associated with both."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "Ten rioters have been sentenced, and five acquitted. (Correct)\nTen new members have been enrolled, and seven resigned. (Incorrect)\nTen new members have been enrolled and seven have resigned. (Correct)"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 168. Carefully distinguish between the verbs lay and lie. The verb lay is transitive and is always followed by an object; the verb lie is intransitive and cannot have an object."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "Lay, laid, laid: Lay the child down to sleep. I laid the book on the table. The hen has laid an egg.\nLie, lay, lain: Let me lie here. He lay under that pipal tree."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "At home, mother says, “Lay the book on the table.” She says lay, because the book is the object. When she rests, she says, “I will lie down for a while.”"
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "At school, the teacher says, “The hen has laid an egg.” He says laid, because lay takes an object — the egg."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 169. An infinitive should be in the present tense, unless it represents action prior to that of the governing verb."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "I should have liked to go (not to have gone).\nHe seems to have enjoyed his stay at Mahabaleshwar. (Correct — the enjoyment was before the seeming.)"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 170. A common blunder is to leave the participle without proper agreement, or with no agreement at all."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "Sitting on the gate, a scorpion stung him. (Incorrect — the participle sitting has no proper subject.)\nSitting on the gate, he was stung by a scorpion. (Correct)\nBeing a very hot day, I remained in my tent. (Incorrect — there is no word for the participle to refer to.)\nAs it was a very hot day, I remained in my tent. (Correct)"
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "In class, Ali writes, “Sitting on the gate, a scorpion stung him.” The teacher says, “Who was sitting on the gate — you or the scorpion? Write: While I was sitting on the gate, a scorpion stung me.”"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 171. Usage, however, permits in certain cases constructions like the following, where the unexpressed subject is indefinite."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "Considering his abilities, he should have done better.\nRoughly speaking, the distance from here to the nearest railway station is two miles.\nTaking everything into consideration, his lot is a happy one."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 172. A present participle should not be used to express an action which is not contemporaneous with the action of the principal verb."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "He sailed for New York on Monday, arriving there on Saturday. (Incorrect)\nHe sailed for New York on Monday, and arrived there on Saturday. (Correct)"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 173. The subjunctive mood is sometimes wrongly used for the indicative. When the statement introduced by if or though is an actual fact, the proper mood is the indicative, not the subjunctive."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "Though the war is over, there is much discontent. (Indicative — a fact)\nIf he was there, he must have heard the talk. (Indicative — a fact)\nIf I were you [but I am not], I should agree. (Subjunctive — mere supposition)"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 174. The verb make is followed by noun or pronoun + plain infinitive (infinitive without to)."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "She made the boy do the whole work. (not: to do the whole work)"
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 175. When used in the passive, make is followed by the to-infinitive."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "The boy was made to do the whole work."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 176. The verbs enjoy, avoid, miss, postpone and suggest are often wrongly used with the to-infinitive. They should be used with the gerund."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "He enjoys swimming. (not: to swim)\nShe avoids meeting people. (not: to meet)\nWe missed seeing the Prime Minister. (not: to see)"
      },
      {
        "t": "p",
        "x": "Note the following common mistakes: (1) Wrong: Suresh told to me about it. Right: Suresh told me about it. The verb tell is followed by an indirect object (me, him, her) without to. (2) Wrong: She told she wouldn’t come. Right: She told me she wouldn’t come. Or: She said she wouldn’t come. When used with a that-clause, tell takes an indirect object, while say does not. (3) Wrong: I want that you should meet him. Right: I want you to meet him. The verb want should not be used with a that-clause. (4) Wrong: I suggest you to apply for the post. Right: I suggest that you (should) apply for the post. The verb suggest should be used with a that-clause; it cannot be used with the to-infinitive."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "At school, Sana says, “I enjoy playing cricket.” She says playing, not to play, because enjoy is followed by a gerund."
      },
      {
        "t": "rl",
        "x": "At home, father says, “I suggest that you read this book.” He does not say “I suggest you to read”, because suggest takes a that-clause."
      },
      {
        "t": "rule",
        "x": "Section 177. The verbs discuss, describe, order and request are transitive verbs. Students often wrongly use these verbs with a preposition."
      },
      {
        "t": "ex",
        "x": "Wrong: We discussed about the matter yesterday. Right: We discussed the matter yesterday.\nWrong: He described about the scenery. Right: He described the scenery.\nWrong: I have ordered for three cups of coffee. Right: I have ordered three cups of coffee.\nWrong: She requested for my help. Right: She requested my help."
      },
      {
        "t": "note",
        "x": "For a detailed treatment of the uses of the Tenses and Auxiliaries, see Chapters 25, 26 and 32 of Book I."
      }
