        "name": "Primary words and compound words",
        "exercises": [
          {
            "label": "The rule (Wren & Martin, Chapter 28, Section 233)",
            "items": [
              {
                "q": "What are Primary Words?",
                "a": "Words which are not derived or compounded or developed from other words. They belong to the original stock of words in the language."
              },
              {
                "q": "In how many ways are new words formed?",
                "a": "In three ways: (i) Compound Words, formed by joining two or more simple words; (ii) Primary Derivatives, formed by making some change in the body of the simple word; (iii) Secondary Derivatives, formed by an addition to the beginning or the end."
              },
              {
                "q": "What is a Prefix and what is a Suffix?",
                "a": "An addition to the beginning of a word is a Prefix; an addition to the end is a Suffix."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "The rule (Wren & Martin, Chapter 28, Sections 234 and 235)",
            "items": [
              {
                "q": "What parts of speech are compound words, for the most part?",
                "a": "Nouns, Adjectives and Verbs."
              },
              {
                "q": "Give two examples each of compound nouns formed from Noun + Noun, Adjective + Noun, Verb + Noun, Gerund + Noun, Adverb + Noun, Verb + Adverb, Adverb + Verb.",
                "a": "Noun + Noun: moonlight, postman. Adjective + Noun: sweetheart, blackboard. Verb + Noun: pickpocket, scarecrow. Gerund + Noun: drawing-room, walking-stick. Adverb + Noun: overcoat, afternoon. Verb + Adverb: drawback, send-off. Adverb + Verb: outset, income."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "label": "The rule (Wren & Martin, Chapter 28, Sections 236 and 237)",
            "items": [
              {
                "q": "Give two examples each of compound adjectives and compound verbs.",
                "a": "Compound Adjectives: blood-red, sky-blue, red-hot, long-suffering. Compound Verbs: waylay, backbite, whitewash, overthrow, foretell, undertake."
              },
              {
                "q": "In most compound words, which word modifies the meaning of the other?",
                "a": "The first word modifies the meaning of the second."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
