        "num": "5",
        "name": "Writing the Essay — Paragraphs, Structure, Style",
        "exercises": [
          {
            "label": "Writing the essay (Chapter 40)",
            "items": [
              {
                "q": "How should an essay be divided?",
                "a": "Every essay should be divided into paragraphs, and each heading should have at least one paragraph to itself. An essay not thus paragraphed looks unattractive and is not easy to read."
              },
              {
                "q": "What is a paragraph?",
                "a": "A paragraph is a group of related sentences that develop a single point."
              },
              {
                "q": "What four principles should be kept in view in constructing a paragraph?",
                "a": "(i) Unity — the paragraph must treat of one subject only. (ii) Variety — paragraphs should not all be of the same length or of the same monotonous structure. (iii) Logical sequence of thought. (iv) Topical sentence — the first sentence often states the subject and is called the topical sentence (or key-sentence); the concluding sentence may sum up effectively what has been said."
              },
              {
                "q": "What are the three parts of an essay?",
                "a": "The Introduction, the Body of the Essay, and the Conclusion."
              },
              {
                "q": "What should the introduction be like?",
                "a": "In a short essay it must be very brief — it would be absurd to have the porch bigger than the building itself. It may be a sentence, or a very short paragraph, but it should always be arresting and pertinent to the subject. It may consist of a definition, a quotation, a proverb, a very brief story, or a general remark leading up to the subject."
              },
              {
                "q": "What is meant by 'proportion' in the body of the essay?",
                "a": "Let each part have due weight given to it. If the subject is 'The good and bad influence of Newspapers', do not devote three-quarters of the essay to good influences and leave only a quarter for the bad."
              },
              {
                "q": "What faults should you avoid in the body of the essay?",
                "a": "Avoid 'padding' and keep to the point. Avoid unnecessary words; in revising, look out for useless repetitions and redundant expressions, and strike them out. Match the words to the sense, and adapt the style to the subject-matter."
              },
              {
                "q": "What may a good conclusion consist of?",
                "a": "(a) A summing up of the arguments of the essay; (b) a final conclusion drawn from the subject-matter; (c) a suitable quotation; (d) a sentence that strikingly expresses the main point you want to drive home."
              },
              {
                "q": "What three words sum up the style a writer should aim at?",
                "a": "Be direct, be simple, be natural. Use short sentences, simple words and constructions, and do not try to imitate any author's style. Never use two words where one, the right one, will do. The secret of clear writing is clear thinking."
              },
              {
                "q": "What is the summary of the method of procedure?",
                "a": "1. Clearly define your subject. 2. Think over it, jot the points down as they occur to you, numbering them. 3. Classify these points in groups under suitable headings, rejecting any that are unsuitable. 4. Arrange these headings in a bare outline. 5. Fill in the outline, making a full outline. 6. Begin to write the essay, dividing it into paragraphs. 7. The essay should consist of introduction, body and conclusion. 8. Write in a simple, concise, clear, direct and natural style."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
