                "a": "Key answers: (a) The poet regards great books as friends who can always be relied upon. Their company delights him. They provide solace when he is in misery. He feels that he is greatly indebted to them. (b) They are the books which contain the minds of the great men of old. (c) The poet's friends are the books. They can never part from him. They have been and will be helpful to him all his life. (d) The poet has often wept with tears of grateful thought, remembering how much he owes to the great writers of the past."
