                "q": "4. 'Oh, sweet content'. (a) Where does the poet seek sweet content? Where does he find it? (b) What striking word-pictures are contained in the poem? (c) Name and explain the figures of speech in the last two lines. (d) Give a suitable title to the poem.",
                "a": "Key answers: (a) The poet seeks sweet content in high and low ranks. He finds it in some quiet place — here in his room, or in a garden on a summer's night. (b) Moths are spoken of as kissing their shadows on the lighted ceiling as they dance around; the dark air blinks with the blind bat's wings, and heaven's bright face twitches with the stars that shine in thousands. (c) The last two lines contain personification: heaven's bright face is represented as twitching with the innumerable stars; and alliteration — the words 'blink', 'blind' and 'bat' begin with the same letter. (d) A suitable title is 'Sweet Content' or 'Contentment'."
