                "a": "Key answers: (a) Keats had read widely in English literature, particularly poetry — he had travelled in the realms of gold and seen many goodly states and kingdoms. (b) Homer is profound and difficult to understand — deep-browed, like a thinker with a heavy brow. (c) The poet felt like a watcher of the skies when a new planet swims into his ken, and like stout Cortez with eagle eyes staring at the Pacific, while his men looked at each other with a wild surmise, silent upon a peak in Darien. (d) A musical line is 'Then felt I like some watcher of the skies / When a new planet swims into his ken.' (e) 'Pure serene' — the pure, calm air of Homer's poetry; 'eagle eyes' — sharp, keen eyes; 'wild surmise' — a wondering guess, full of astonishment."
